                "To survive within this blistering budget, enterprise ad tech architectures deploy three mandatory network optimizations: First, 'Persistent Socket Pooling and Keep-Alive Connections': establishing new TCP and TLS handshakes for each auction introduces 30-50ms of pure overhead; SSPs and DSPs maintain pre-warmed persistent connection pools across private peering fabrics. Second, 'Edge-Terminated Geo-Routing': DSP bidding engines are co-located in the identical cloud regions (e.g., AWS us-east-1, GCP us-central1) as the major ad exchanges to minimize speed-of-light optical transit delay. Third, 'Circuit Breakers and Adaptive Throttling': if a DSP's internal queuing latency spikes above 15ms, automated circuit breakers shed non-essential ML features or reject low-value inventory to keep core bidding operations responsive.",
                "At the Linux operating system level, bidding clusters tune kernel network parameters to sustain multi-million QPS loads. Systems engineers configure 'SO_REUSEPORT' on incoming socket listeners to distribute traffic evenly across multi-core CPU architectures without lock contention. They disable Nagle's algorithm by setting 'TCP_NODELAY', forcing packets to transmit immediately without buffering, and deploy modern congestion control algorithms such as Google's Bottleneck Bandwidth and RTT (BBR) to maintain maximum bandwidth across transcontinental interconnects.",
